8.3.3
Computing the Sharpe ratio of the strategies
As discussed in Appendix (D) a discrete time series is a set of observations xt, each one being recorded at a speciﬁed
ﬁxed time interval. One can assume that each observation xt is a realised value of a certain random variable Xt. That
is, the time series txt , t P T0u is a realisation of the family of random variables tXt , t P T0u. Hence, we can model
the data as a realisation of a stochastic process tXt , t P Tu where T  T0. Given the deﬁnition of the Sharp ratio in
